# daq-mcp engineering rules

## Backend boundary

MCP tools in `server.py` must never import or call `nidaqmx` directly. All
hardware access goes through `daq_mcp.backend.get_backend()` and the
`DAQBackend` interface. That boundary keeps the server testable without NI
drivers and lets simulation and real hardware share one tool surface.

If you need a new hardware capability, add it to `DAQBackend` first, implement
it in both backends (or explicitly document why simulation cannot support it),
then call it from the tool.

## Allowlist every channel argument

Any new tool that accepts a channel name must call `_check(channel)` (or an
equivalent allowlist gate) before touching the backend. Writes must also
respect `WRITES_ENABLED` / `DAQ_MCP_ALLOW_WRITE`. Do not add a bypass "just
for debugging" in committed code.

Analog output must clamp to `AO_VOLTAGE_LIMITS` and return both requested and
applied values plus a `clamped` flag. Digital writes must return hardware
read-back, not an echo of the requested value.

## Do not guess the nidaqmx API

Before writing or changing real-backend calls, verify them against the current
nidaqmx documentation (task creation, channel naming, terminal configuration,
timing, error types). Do not invent method names or parameter shapes from
memory — plausible-looking wrong calls fail only on hardware.

If a call is uncertain, say so and look it up. Prefer one Task per operation,
created and closed inside the call, for every *snapshot* tool. Continuous
acquisition (`start_stream` / `LiveMonitor`) is the deliberate exception: one
long-lived task owned by the monitor thread, with snapshot tools reading that
channel from the buffer instead of opening a competing task. Catch
`nidaqmx.errors.DaqError` and return a structured `{error, error_code}` dict
instead of leaking a traceback — except inside the stream drain path, where
only "samples not ready yet" is ignored and every other code stops the monitor.
